Q: How to validate that a string is of a desired value with integers in certain positions in C? A: <blockquote>
<p>a second opinion to see if my implementation works</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It does not.</p>
<p><code>fgets(buffer, size, stdin)</code> reads up to <code>size - 1</code> characters and then appends a <em>null character</em> to the buffer.</p>
<p>Consider:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>printf(&quot;Enter the task's due date (DD/MM/YYYY):\n&quot;);
    if (fgets(aux_date, 10, stdin) == NULL){
</code></pre>
<p>OP's code instead attempts to read an 11 character text input like <code>&quot;01/02/2026\n&quot;</code> (which needs a buffer of at least 12 to store.)</p>
<p>Further, if the line input is too long, only a partial amount is read.</p>
<p>Instead allocate a buffer, perhaps 2x the anticipated needs size to consume extra.</p>
<p>Also perform testing in a logical sequence:<br />
Did we get the line?,<br />
Is it numeric with expected separators?<br />
Range of values <em>roughly</em> OK?<br />
Did we reject other spurious dates like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Year_1900_problem#Microsoft_Excel" rel="nofollow noreferrer">29/02/1900</a> (not done below)?</p>
<p>Sample simplified code:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#define LINE_SZ_MAX 100
char buf[LINE_SZ_MAX * 2];

if (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, stdin) == NULL) {
  // No more input (or rare input error)
  ...
  exit(-1);
}

size_t len = strlen(buf);
if (len + 1 == sizeof buf) {
  // Input was too long.
  // We could take evasive action, but for simplicity,
  // just bail out with hostile input.
  ...
  exit(-1);
}

struct tm tm = { 0 }; // Zero all members.

// Parse input for date.
// For simplicity, tolerate optional leading white space and signs.
// We could use stronger tests for spaces and signs, perhaps add later.
// Unless truly needed, consider being tolerant.
int n = 0;
sscanf(buf, &quot;%2d/%2d/%4d %n&quot;, &amp;tm.tm_mday, &amp;tm.tm_mon, &amp;tm.tm_year, &amp;n);
if (n == 0 || buf[n] != '\0') {
  // Invalid numeric input
  ...
}

// Perform some range tests.  Augment as desired.
if (tm.tm_day &lt; 1 || tm.tm_mday &gt; 31 || tm.tm_mon &lt; 1 || tm.tm_mon &gt; 12) {
  // Invalid date
  ...
}

printf(&quot;Date: %02d/%02d/%04d\n&quot;, tm.tm_mday, tm.tm_mon, tm.tm_year);

// Adjust to struct tm members epochs.
tm.tm_mon--;  // Months since January
tm.tm_year -= 1900  // Years since 1900
</code></pre>

Q: How to create precise platformer movement using MatterJS? A: <p>It's certainly possible to use MJS for platformers. Here are a number of examples to illustrate how it typically behaves:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://codepen.io/tanker837/pen/qaeQKZ" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Tanker837 side scroller</a></li>
<li><a href="https://codepen.io/Jason-Leisure/pen/ZYWOQbx" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Jason Leisure platformer</a></li>
<li><a href="https://codepen.io/FullR/pen/QqPggr" rel="nofollow noreferrer">James Meyers simple platformer POC</a></li>
<li><a href="https://decodingnature.nyuadim.com/2024/11/10/jump-game-with-matter-js-library-week-10/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Jump game</a></li>
<li><a href="https://gist.github.com/lilgreenland/c6f4b78a78b73dc8b1f8fa650d617b85" rel="nofollow noreferrer">lilgreenland platformer</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/68425122/6243352">Minimal platformer-like starter</a> (without jumping)</li>
</ul>
<p>But as mentioned in the comments, Matter.js is potentially not the optimal tool for the type of hard-stop/partial physics platformer you want to make. MJS is best suited to fluid physics with messiness, like ramps, and anything where you'd want a full Verlet integration engine.</p>
<p>MJS object pushes are possible, but <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/59321773/prevent-force-dragging-bodies-through-other-bodies-with-matterjs">objects will penetrate one another</a> and tend to be sloppy/imprecise.</p>
<p>You ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does anyone know of a physics library that might be better for my use case then? All I really care about is gravity and resolving collisions</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For a partial physics platformer, Phaser in arcade mode would simplify the code dramatically:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const config = {
  type: Phaser.AUTO,
  width: 500,
  height: 200,
  physics: {
    default: "arcade",
    arcade: {
      gravity: {y: 600},
      debug: true
    }
  },
  scene: {create, update}
};
new Phaser.Game(config);
let player, cursors;

function create() {
  cursors = this.input.keyboard.createCursorKeys();

  const platforms = this.physics.add.staticGroup();
  platforms.create(250, 190)
    .setDisplaySize(500, 20)
    .refreshBody();
  platforms.create(300, 120)
    .setDisplaySize(140, 14)
    .refreshBody();

  player = this.physics.add.sprite(60, 100)
    .setSize(18, 18)
    .setCollideWorldBounds(true);
  this.physics.add.collider(player, platforms);
}

function update() {
  // Dead-stop horizontal movement
  player.setVelocityX(0);

  if (cursors.left.isDown &amp;&amp; !cursors.right.isDown) {
    player.setVelocityX(-160);
  }
  else if (cursors.right.isDown &amp;&amp; !cursors.left.isDown) {
    player.setVelocityX(160);
  }

  // Jump only if on ground
  if (cursors.up.isDown &amp;&amp; player.body.blocked.down) {
    player.setVelocityY(-300);
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.as-console-wrapper{display:none!important}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/phaser@3.90.0/dist/phaser.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Pushing objects is totally doable with Phaser:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const config = {
  type: Phaser.AUTO,
  width: 500,
  height: 200,
  physics: {
    default: "arcade",
    arcade: {
      gravity: {y: 600},
      debug: true
    }
  },
  scene: {create, update}
};
new Phaser.Game(config);
let player, box, cursors;

function create() {
  cursors = this.input.keyboard.createCursorKeys();

  const platforms = this.physics.add.staticGroup();
  platforms.create(250, 190)
    .setDisplaySize(500, 20)
    .refreshBody();
  platforms.create(300, 120)
    .setDisplaySize(140, 14)
    .refreshBody();

  player = this.physics.add.sprite(60, 100)
    .setSize(18, 18)
    .setCollideWorldBounds(true);
  player.body.setMass(1);

  box = this.physics.add.sprite(200, 100)
    .setSize(20, 20)
    .setCollideWorldBounds(true);
  box.body.setMass(2);          // heavier than player
  box.body.setDragX(1200);      // avoid sliding
  box.body.setFriction(1, 0);   // resist sideways motion

  this.physics.add.collider(player, platforms);
  this.physics.add.collider(box, platforms);

  // Let the player push the box
  this.physics.add.collider(player, box);
}

function update() {
  // Dead-stop horizontal movement
  player.setVelocityX(0);

  if (cursors.left.isDown &amp;&amp; !cursors.right.isDown) {
    player.setVelocityX(-160);
  }
  else if (cursors.right.isDown &amp;&amp; !cursors.left.isDown) {
    player.setVelocityX(160);
  }

  // Jump only if player is on ground
  if (cursors.up.isDown &amp;&amp; player.body.blocked.down) {
    player.setVelocityY(-300);
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.as-console-wrapper{display:none!important}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/phaser@3.90.0/dist/phaser.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Here's a comparable app in MJS, extending <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/68416755/keyboard-movement-is-jerky-in-matter-js/68425122#68425122">my demo above</a>, which operates at a lower level and requires a good deal of work to achieve a similar result:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const {Engine, Render, Runner, Bodies, Body, Composite, Events} = Matter;

const engine = Engine.create();
engine.world.gravity.y = 2;

const render = Render.create({
  element: document.body,
  engine,
  options: {width: 500, height: 200, wireframes: true},
});
Render.run(render);
Runner.run(Runner.create(), engine);

const player = Bodies.rectangle(60, 100, 18, 18, {
  inertia: Infinity,
  friction: 0,
  frictionAir: 0.05,
});

const box = Bodies.rectangle(200, 170, 20, 20, {
  friction: 0.3,
  frictionAir: 0.05,
  restitution: 0.5,
  density: 0.0001,
});

const ground = Bodies.rectangle(250, 190, 500, 20, {
  isStatic: true,
});
const platform = Bodies.rectangle(300, 120, 140, 14, {
  isStatic: true,
});
const leftWall = Bodies.rectangle(0, 100, 20, 200, {
  isStatic: true,
});
const rightWall = Bodies.rectangle(500, 100, 20, 200, {
  isStatic: true,
});

const groundSensor = Bodies.rectangle(250, 180, 500, 2, {
  isStatic: true,
  isSensor: true,
});
const platformSensor = Bodies.rectangle(300, 110, 140, 2, {
  isStatic: true,
  isSensor: true,
});

Composite.add(engine.world, [
  player,
  box,
  ground,
  platform,
  leftWall,
  rightWall,
  groundSensor,
  platformSensor,
]);

const keysDown = new Set();
document.addEventListener("keydown", e =&gt; keysDown.add(e.code));
document.addEventListener("keyup", e =&gt; keysDown.delete(e.code));

const touchingSensors = new Set();
Events.on(engine, "collisionStart", event =&gt; {
  for (const pair of event.pairs) {
    if (pair.bodyA === player &amp;&amp; pair.bodyB.isSensor) {
      touchingSensors.add(pair.bodyB.id);
    }

    if (pair.bodyB === player &amp;&amp; pair.bodyA.isSensor) {
      touchingSensors.add(pair.bodyA.id);
    }
  }
});
Events.on(engine, "collisionEnd", event =&gt; {
  for (const pair of event.pairs) {
    if (pair.bodyA === player &amp;&amp; pair.bodyB.isSensor) {
      touchingSensors.delete(pair.bodyB.id);
    }

    if (pair.bodyB === player &amp;&amp; pair.bodyA.isSensor) {
      touchingSensors.delete(pair.bodyA.id);
    }
  }
});

const isGrounded = () =&gt; touchingSensors.size &gt; 0;

const keyHandlers = {
  ArrowRight: () =&gt; {
    const force = isGrounded() ? 0.0005 : 0.0005;
    Body.applyForce(player, player.position, {x: force, y: 0});
  },
  ArrowLeft: () =&gt; {
    const force = isGrounded() ? 0.0005 : 0.0005;
    Body.applyForce(player, player.position, {x: -force, y: 0});
  },
  ArrowUp: () =&gt; {
    if (isGrounded()) {
      Body.setVelocity(player, {x: player.velocity.x, y: -15});
    }
  },
};

Events.on(engine, "beforeUpdate", () =&gt; {
  keysDown.forEach(k =&gt; keyHandlers[k]?.());

  if (
    isGrounded() &amp;&amp;
    !keysDown.has("ArrowLeft") &amp;&amp;
    !keysDown.has("ArrowRight")
  ) {
    Body.setVelocity(player, {x: 0, y: player.velocity.y});
  }
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/matter-js@0.20.0/build/matter.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Even here, you can see it'll take a good deal of tweaking to get it close to the precision the Phaser arcade engine has out of the box.</p>
<p>Another consideration is the way you want the game to look. All of these examples use the debugging renderer. If you're using Matter.js and want any non-trivial visual behavior, like a sprite sheet, you need to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/65207865/matter-js-is-there-any-way-to-animate-my-sprite/65301374#65301374">implement it yourself</a> or <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/77436034/6243352">plug Matter.js into a rendering library like Pixi.js</a>. But Phaser has &quot;batteries included&quot; sprites and visual rendering that will work with minimal hassle.</p>
<p>In general, there's somewhat of a misconception that MJS is a good library for all things that involve collisions or physics, but this isn't the case. MJS is best for simulations involving loose objects falling/shooting around and anything you see in the set of <a href="https://brm.io/matter-js/demo/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">provided demos</a>. Pushing it beyond those demos, trying to simulate accurate real-world physics, or trying to get bodies to behave predictably, or adding non-trivial rendering typically ends in a great deal of frustration.</p>
<p>That said, if Matter.js can be wrangled into giving you the behavior you want, it can potentially provide a more interesting physics experience than Phaser. <a href="https://docs.phaser.io/api-documentation/class/physics-matter-matterphysics" rel="nofollow noreferrer">You can also combine the two</a> if you'd like to use Phaser for the game framework features it provides along with MJS for physics.</p>

Q: Can&#39;t capture every emittion from StateFlow when testing with Turbine A: <p>UnconfinedTestDispatcher, your setStates() function runs so fast that all five updates happen before Turbine's collector can catch them. StateFlow only keeps the latest value, so you only see isLoading = true (the last one).
The Fix:
Use StandardTestDispatcher with advanceUntilIdle():</p>
<pre><code>@Test
fun `should capture all state changes`() = runTest {
    val testDispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler)
    Dispatchers.setMain(testDispatcher)

    val viewModel = HomeViewModel(...)

    viewModel.uiState.test {
        awaitItem() // initial state

        viewModel.setStates()
        advanceUntilIdle() // &lt;-- This is the key!

        assertEquals(true, awaitItem().isLoading)
        assertEquals(false, awaitItem().isLoading)
        assertEquals(true, awaitItem().isLoading)
        assertEquals(false, awaitItem().isLoading)
        assertEquals(true, awaitItem().isLoading)

        cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()
    }

    Dispatchers.resetMain()
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>make each state update happen in a way that gives stateIn a chance to process them. Instead of launching everything in one coroutine, yield control between updates</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>kotlinfun setStates() {
    viewModelScope.launch {
        updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = true) }
        yield() // Let other coroutines run
        updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = false) }
        yield()
        updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = true) }
        yield()
        updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = false) }
        yield()
        updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = true) }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or use this test approach:</p>
<pre><code>kotlin@Test
fun `should capture all state changes`() = runTest {
    val testDispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler)
    Dispatchers.setMain(testDispatcher)

    val viewModel = HomeViewModel(...)

    viewModel.uiState.test {
        awaitItem() // initial state

        // Trigger each update separately with advancement in between
        viewModel.updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = true) }
        advanceUntilIdle()
        assertEquals(true, awaitItem().isLoading)

        viewModel.updateStateFlow { copy(isLoading = false) }
        advanceUntilIdle()
        assertEquals(false, awaitItem().isLoading)

        // ... repeat for each update

        cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()
    }

    Dispatchers.resetMain()
}
</code></pre>
<p>The core issue is that stateIn needs the dispatcher to actually schedule/run the collection coroutine between your updates. When all updates happen synchronously in one block, conflation is unavoidable with StateFlow.</p>

Q: How to correctly pass dynamically built command arguments to exec.Command in Go? A: <p>The issue is how you call <code>exec.Command</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>cmd := exec.Command(docker_containers_string_splitted[0],
    docker_containers_string_splitted...)
</code></pre>
<p><code>exec.Command(name, args...)</code> expects <strong>args without the executable name</strong>.
You’re passing <code>&quot;docker&quot;</code> twice, so the command becomes:</p>
<pre><code>docker docker compose up -d nginx &quot;&quot;
</code></pre>
<p><code>docker</code> treats <code>docker</code> as the subcommand, so <code>-d</code> is seen at the top level and you get <code>unknown shorthand flag: 'd'</code>.</p>
<p>You also get a trailing empty arg (<code>&quot;&quot;</code>) because you split a string that ends with a space.</p>
<h3>Correct approach: build args as a slice</h3>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>args := []string{&quot;compose&quot;}

if docker_command == &quot;up&quot; {
    args = append(args, &quot;up&quot;, &quot;-d&quot;)
} else {
    args = append(args, docker_command)
}

for name := range config.Services {
    if !slices.Contains(excluded_container_list, name) {
        args = append(args, name)
    }
}

fmt.Printf(&quot;[DEBUG] Executing: docker %v\n&quot;, args)

cmd := exec.Command(&quot;docker&quot;, args...)
output, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()
</code></pre>
<h3>If you still want to split a string</h3>
<p>Use <code>strings.Fields</code>, not <code>strings.Split</code>, to avoid empty args:</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>fields := strings.Fields(docker_containers_string)
cmd := exec.Command(fields[0], fields[1:]...)
</code></pre>
<p>The slice-based approach is the normal and safest way to do this.</p>

Q: &quot;var canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;)&quot; can&#39;t be a global variable? A: <p>&lt;script&gt;</p>
<pre><code>const canvas = document.getElementById('game');

const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

let gravity = 0.6;

let gameSpeed = 4; // Initial game speed

const player = {

    x: 80,

    y: 300,

    width: 40,

    height: 50,

    yVel: 0,

    jump() {

        if (this.y \&gt;= 300) {

            this.yVel = -12;

        }

    },

    update() {

        this.yVel += gravity;

        this.y += this.yVel;

        if (this.y \&gt; 300) {

            this.y = 300;

            this.yVel = 0;

        }

    },

    draw() {

        ctx.fillStyle = '#ff4757';

        ctx.fillRect(this.x, this.y, this.width, this.height);

    }

};

let obstacles = \[\];

let frame = 0;

let score = 0;

function spawnObstacle() {

    obstacles.push({

        x: canvas.width,

        y: 320,

        width: 30,

        height: 30

    });

}

function updateObstacles() {

    obstacles.forEach(o =\&gt; o.x -= gameSpeed);

    obstacles = obstacles.filter(o =\&gt; o.x + o.width \&gt; 0);

}

function drawObstacles() {

    ctx.fillStyle = '#2f3542';

    obstacles.forEach(o =\&gt; ctx.fillRect(o.x, o.y, o.width, o.height));

}

function detectCollision() {

    for (let o of obstacles) {

        if (

            player.x \&lt; o.x + o.width &amp;&amp;

            player.x + player.width \&gt; o.x &amp;&amp;

            player.y \&lt; o.y + o.height &amp;&amp;

            player.y + player.height \&gt; o.y

        ) {

            return true;

        }

    }

    return false;

}

function drawGround() {

    ctx.fillStyle = '#2ed573';

    ctx.fillRect(0, 350, canvas.width, 50);

}

function drawScore() {

    ctx.fillStyle = '#000';

    ctx.font = '20px Arial';

    ctx.fillText('Score: ' + score, 20, 30);

}

function gameLoop() {

    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

    drawGround();

    player.update();

    player.draw();

    // Increase game speed every 200 frames for increasing difficulty

    if (frame % 200 === 0) {

        gameSpeed += 0.5;

    }

    if (frame % 120 === 0) spawnObstacle();

    updateObstacles();

    drawObstacles();

    score++;

    drawScore();

    if (detectCollision()) {

        ctx.fillStyle = '#000';

        ctx.font = '40px Arial';

        ctx.fillText('Game Over', 280, 200);

        return;

    }

    frame++;

    requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);

}

window.addEventListener('keydown', e =\&gt; {

    if (e.code === 'Space') player.jump();

});

gameLoop();
</code></pre>
<p>&lt;/script&gt;</p>
